= Initializing Sybase ASE 11.0.3.3 on Linux =

After installing Sybase Adaptive Server Enterprise, you can initialize the server by following these steps.

First, //su// to the sybase user using the -l option.

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
su -l sybase
}}}
}}}

Run '''install/sybinit''', a text-based installation program. When prompted, follow these instructions.

select '''3. Configure a Server product'''[[br]]select '''1. SQL Server'''[[br]]select '''1. Configure a new Adaptive Server'''[[br]]type ctrl-a to accept the default server name[[br]][[br]]select '''1. CONFIGURE SERVER'S INTERFACES FILE ENTRY'''[[br]]select '''3. Add a new listener service'''[[br]]don't modify the default hostname/address[[br]]select '''2. Port:'''[[br]]enter 4100[[br]]type ctrl-a to '''Accept and Continue'''[[br]]type y when prompted with '''Is this information correct?'''[[br]]type ctrl-a to '''Accept and Continue'''[[br]]type y when prompted with '''Write the changes to the interfaces file now?'''[[br]][[br]]select '''2. MASTER DEVICE CONFIGURATION'''[[br]]type ctrl-a to '''Accept and Continue'''[[br]]Press &lt;return&gt; to continue, ignoring all WARNING's. [[br]]select '''3. SYBSYSTEMPROCS DATABASE CONFIGURATION'''[[br]]type ctrl-a to '''Accept and Continue'''[[br]][[br]]select '''4. SET ERRORLOG LOCATION'''[[br]]type ctrl-a to '''Accept and Continue'''[[br]][[br]]select '''5. CONFIGURE DEFAULT BACKUP SERVER'''[[br]]type ctrl-a to '''Accept and Continue'''[[br]][[br]]select '''6. CONFIGURE LANGUAGES'''[[br]]type ctrl-a to '''Accept and Continue'''[[br]][[br]]select '''7. CONFIGURE CHARACTER SETS'''[[br]]type ctrl-a to '''Accept and Continue'''[[br]][[br]]select '''8. CONFIGURE SORT ORDER'''[[br]]type ctrl-a to '''Accept and Continue'''[[br]][[br]]select '''9. CONFIGURE AUDITING'''[[br]]type ctrl-a to '''Accept and Continue'''[[br]][[br]]on the '''SYBASE SERVER CONFIGURATION''' screen, type ctrl-a to '''Accept and Continue'''[[br]]type y when prompted with '''Execute the Sybase Server Configuration now?'''[[br]]Press &lt;return&gt; to continue, ignoring all WARNING's.

If all goes well, you should see several '''Running task:''' and '''Task succeeded:''' messages. When you get a '''Configuration completed successfully''' message, press &lt;return&gt; to continue.

type ctrl-x to '''Exit Screen'''[[br]]type ctrl-x to '''Exit Screen'''[[br]]type ctrl-x to '''Exit Screen'''

== Starting the Database at Boot Time ==

Replace ///usr/local/sybase// with whatever directory Sybase ASE is installed in.

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
 #!/bin/sh

export SYBASE=/usr/local/sybase

export PATH=$PATH:$SYBASE/bin:$SYBASE/install

case "$1" in
        start)
                for i in `ls $SYBASE/install/RUN_*`
                do
                        su -c "startserver -f $i" sybase > /dev/null 2>&1;
                done
                ;;
        stop)
                kill `ps -efa | grep sybase | grep -v grep | awk '{print $2}'`
                ;;
        *)
                echo $"Usage: $0 {start|stop}"
                exit 1
esac

exit 0
}}}
}}}

Install this script and run it with the "start" option to start up the database. Running it with the "stop" option shuts the database down. To access a database, it must be running.

